# Premise decomposition: A fixed review rule can use lessons from new incidents only if it can identify when they apply

**Register:** claim
**Central commitment (one sentence):** A standing instruction such as "check past incidents" can stay fixed while new incidents add lessons, but those lessons only help if someone can identify when an earlier failure's relevant feature is present in a new case, so the incident changes what the review must distinguish, not the rule.

## Premises and counterexamples

The note has two parts. One is a necessary condition, in the title: lessons are usable only if their applicability can be identified. The other is a rider in the closing sentence: given that condition, the rule itself need not change. The worked example (shared-origin citations) supports the necessary condition well. Its two structural premises hold: applicability is structural, and a source-name lookup misses it. The possibility claim that a reviewer could recognize the pattern also holds.

The pressure is on the rider and on what the note leaves unstated. The rider needs three things the note does not state:

- The relevant incident reaches the reviewer. At scale, this needs retrieval by mechanism, which the bare rule does not supply.
- One incident fixes a single lesson.
- The identifying capacity sits in the reviewer, not in the incident record or the rule.

The title and body also disagree about who does the identifying: "it" (the rule) in the title, the reviewer in the body. Each of these failures can be repaired by stating a condition. None breaks the necessary-condition mechanism, so all are LOCAL. The strongest repair would bound the "rule need not change" rider to reviewers who can survey or mechanism-retrieve the incident record.

## For the human

Look first at the closing sentence, "not that the review rule must change". It holds only if the relevant incident reaches the reviewer. As incidents accumulate, that requires mechanism-level indexing or a changed rule, which is the opposite of what the sentence says.
